```

Wait — the `sendIfNotSuppressed` helper is unused; let me simplify. Also the `send` guard: `if (!send) return;` — if no onMediaEvent hook (headless tests), don't attach listeners (but still need the state for suppression? no). Actually for tests without a transport, attaching listeners is harmless. But the encode functions are imported — fine. Let me keep attaching even without send (state used by apply). Actually — if `send` is null, the listeners send nothing. Let me attach regardless and guard each send. Simpler.

Also — `timeupdate` suppression: only report while playing? The timeupdate fires only when the media is playing (and seeking). Reporting while paused-but-seeking — a seek sets currentTime and fires timeupdate; suppressed by suppressTimeUntil. When playing, timeupdate fires ~4x/sec. That's the app's position updates. Fine.

Let me also reconsider: `media.currentTime` on timeupdate — the client sends the current position. The app sets position State → re-emits MEDIA_POSITION → applyMediaProperty sets currentTime again → but it's the same value → no actual seek → no timeupdate → no loop. But applyMediaProperty sets suppressTimeUntil each time → subsequent real timeupdates within 250ms suppressed → could starve position updates if MEDIA_POSITION is re-emitted frequently. But MEDIA_POSITION re-emits only when position State CHANGES (signal equality). The app's position changes only when: (a) timeupdate sets it (to the audio's currentTime — which equals what we just set via MEDIA_POSITION... equality → no re-emit → no re-apply → no suppress reset). So the loop is broken by equality. Good.

But wait — there's a subtle issue: the app sets position from timeupdate (value V1). This re-emits MEDIA_POSITION=V1 → applyMediaProperty sets currentTime=V1 → suppressTimeUntil=now+250. Then the audio continues playing; the next timeupdate at V1+0.25 fires — suppressed (within 250ms). The next after that fires — sends V1+0.5 → app sets position → re-emits → apply currentTime... So every other timeupdate is suppressed. That's fine — position updates at ~2/sec. Acceptable.

Actually — to reduce re-emit churn: the app should only send MEDIA_POSITION back when the user seeks (slider drag), not when timeupdate sets it. But the signal is the same State — the emitter can't distinguish. The equality suppression handles it (no re-emit when unchanged). So the flow is: timeupdate → position=V1 → (already V1 from a prior emit? no) → re-emit MEDIA_POSITION=V1 → apply currentTime=V1 (no-op, same value) + suppress 250ms. This means after EVERY timeupdate, a MEDIA_POSITION re-emit + a currentTime set (no-op) + 250ms suppression. That's a lot of churn (a SET_PROPERTY every ~250ms while playing). Not ideal but acceptable for a demo.

